throttle and debounce function 

Absolutely! Debounce and Throttle are two key frontend techniques to control function execution rate, especially for optimizing expensive operations like:

scroll, resize, mousemove, input

API calls

Button click spam

Let’s look at both with clear definitions, code, and examples 👇

🧠 Debounce vs Throttle (Quick Summary)
Feature	Debounce	Throttle
Definition	Wait after last call	Allow 1 call per time window
Example	Search input field	Window resize, scroll
Behavior	Bunch of calls → 1 final execution	Call → wait → next call

🛑 Debounce
Delays execution until a function hasn’t been called for delay ms.

✅ Code
js
Copy
Edit
function debounce(fn, delay) {
  let timer;
  return function (...args) {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), delay);
  };
}
📦 Usage Example
js
Copy
Edit
function fetchSuggestions(query) {
  console.log("API Call:", query);
}

const input = document.querySelector("input");
input.addEventListener("input", debounce((e) => {
  fetchSuggestions(e.target.value);
}, 500));
✅ Behavior:
User types: v, vi, vin, vina → only 1 API call after 500ms pause.

🚦 Throttle
Ensures function runs at most once every delay ms, no matter how many times it's triggered.

✅ Code
js
Copy
Edit
function throttle(fn, delay) {
  let lastCall = 0;
  return function (...args) {
    const now = Date.now();
    if (now - lastCall >= delay) {
      lastCall = now;
      fn.apply(this, args);
    }
  };
}
📦 Usage Example
js
Copy
Edit
function handleScroll() {
  console.log("Scrolled at", Date.now());
}

window.addEventListener("scroll", throttle(handleScroll, 200));
✅ Behavior:
Even if scroll fires 50 times/second, handleScroll() runs once every 200ms.

🧪 Demo Timeline Comparison
Events fired at: 0ms, 50ms, 100ms, 200ms, 400ms

Debounce(200ms): fires once, at 600ms (after final event)

Throttle(200ms): fires at 0ms, 200ms, 400ms

📌 When to Use What?
Use Case	Use
Live search/API input	Debounce
Window resize	Throttle
Scroll-based loading	Throttle
Button spam block	Debounce




